Raw-eMMC panic log dump from the halt hook + self-contained SMP timeout line.

(1) SMP bring-up panics happen before onc_emmc_init() starts the 0132 SWEETLOG
    flusher, so the panic text only flashes on the panel.  0147 makes the 0144
    PE_halt_restart hook write the 0129 console ring to eMMC itself, before the
    reset, using 0146's lock-free early raw-SDHCI path (ml_io_map + PIO, no
    IOKit/threads/locks).

    Layout is exactly the one 0132 uses at the end of userdata:
      block 0 (4 KiB): "SWEETLOG", u32 version=1, u32 ring size=4 MiB,
                       u64 total console bytes, u64 seq
      blocks 1..1024 (4 KiB each): the console ring, byte n at n % size
    so `xnuios/sweet-onc/tools/sweet-log.py` decodes it unchanged.
    To stay bounded on the 400 kHz legacy link the dump writes the header plus
    the NEWEST `sweetpanicdumpkb` KiB of the ring (default 32, max 4096) - the
    panic lines are at the tail, so they are always included.  The old header
    is read first so `seq` keeps incrementing.  `sweetpanicdump=0` disables.
    Any failure prints a reason and returns (the reset continues).

(2) `SweetCPU::startCPU()` stores the PSCI CPU_ON parameters in globals and the
    5 s SMP timeout IOLog/panic prints them, so one line is self-contained:
      cpu=1 target=0x1 entry=0x... rc=0 mark=... stage=... init=... sig=...

